{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794599174,"nonce":"odN8ptSLvnvHXTIWvPl4iNgM0G4=","message":"Mid-run gaps, followed up: 48 runs of 30 s, then a stall watchdog (heartbeat from new_events and about_to_wait; on 100 ms of silence it logs the loop state and takes a 1 ms stack sample of the process; removed afterwards).\n\n8 gaps of 114 to 967 ms in the first 24 runs. In every one the frames either side cost 0.6 to 3.5 ms and the gap sits between two frame callbacks, so no slow phase. With the watchdog, two mid-run stalls were caught: Focused(false) 53 ms before one, Occluded(true) 162 ms before the other, which sat 115 ms inside a Frame handler. Samples of the main thread put it in CAMetalLayer nextDrawable (usleep poll) and in CAMetalDrawable create and release (mach_msg to the window server). The timer thread was on time throughout.\n\nSo the wake is not late: the main thread is blocked in the frame path on window server IPC, and that is what the 0.3 to 1 s gaps are. A window covered by a second copy of the example kept rendering at 20 to 44 fps for 13 s, which is the part that can be changed; filed as f9170ae with the proposed handling of Occluded. The 0.3 to 1 s stalls themselves did not reproduce on demand, only ~110 ms ones at an occlusion transition.\n\nClosing: the late-wake question is answered (startup activation, then window server stalls in the frame path), and what is left to do is in f9170ae.","files":null}]}